{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\n\nCalibration finding (2026-07-27/28), grounded in the persisted REVIEW_RESULT corpus\n(51,964 verified findings; 18,085 on plan-v3):\n\nA G6 finding on epic c4ad-a93e-0613-408f (sec-semantic-layer-2026-challenge) described a\nmechanism that would NOT work — four sites branching on the literal `\"s3vectors\"` omitted from\nthe plan's scope, which would have re-embedded the whole corpus on every container boot and\nrouted a wipe to a LanceDB drop against Postgres. It scored `impact=0.0`, `severity=none`, and\nlanded ADVISORY despite `validity=0.8889`, `blocking_enabled=true`, `block_threshold=0.60`.\n\nRoot cause is NOT the threshold (0.60 is already the most permissive blocking bar in the\nregistry). `impact_plan` is a MAX over seven axes; the verifier graded all seven `none`, so\n`max(...)=0.0`. The axes that recorded the real harm (`prod_impact: medium`,\n`blast_radius: module`, `likelihood: medium`) are deliberately unread by `impact_plan` — they\nare the superseded mean model's inputs, retained only for sidecar continuity.\n\nThe axis that SHOULD have fired is `divergent_implementation` (\"the plan diverges from the\nimplementation/reality it claims to describe\"). Corpus evidence that it is mis-calibrated:\n\n- It fires on only 7.72% of plan-v3 findings, and it is the ONLY hard-override axis never\n  calibrated (born in d487060b5, one line of prose, never revisited). Its sibling\n  `ac_unverifiable` got a closed `Literal` + worked examples in story\n  large-sleepful-needlefish; this axis did not.\n- Of 1,307 findings in the \"omitted scope site / unenumerated consumer\" class — the family the\n  axis exists to describe — it is graded `none` **1,173 times (~90%)**. 770 of those have all\n  seven axes `none`, i.e. impact exactly 0.0.\n- 47.7% of ALL plan-v3 findings (8,632) have all seven axes `none` → impact 0.0. This is the\n  single largest population in the corpus, not an outlier.\n\n## What\n\nTwo changes, landing together under ONE impact-model version bump (plan-v4), because the bump\ncloses the plan-v3 calibration cohort and we must not spend two.\n\n### 1. Grade `divergent_implementation` by DIVERGENCE KIND (the approved fix)\n\nMirror the oracle-kind precedent exactly — replace the ordinal `none|low|medium|high` with a\nclosed `Literal` set that separates a load-bearing divergence from a cosmetic one:\n\n- `contradicts_reality` — the plan asserts something about the code/system that is FALSE\n  (a named symbol/file/behavior does not exist as described, or exists differently).\n  KEEPS the 0.85 hard floor.\n- `omits_required_site` — the plan's scope/file list omits a site the change provably MUST\n  touch, where omitting it changes runtime behavior or leaves the goal unmet. KEEPS the 0.85\n  hard floor. (This is the class the G6 finding belongs to, and the ~90%-missed family.)\n- `incomplete_enumeration` — a site is omitted but touching it is optional/cosmetic (docs,\n  comments, a redundant mention); the goal still holds without it. Contributes\n  DIVERGENCE_INCOMPLETE_CONTRIB, BELOW every blocking threshold, and NEVER floors — coached,\n  not auto-blocked.\n- `none` — does not apply.\n\n`DIVERGENCE_INCOMPLETE_CONTRIB` is pinned by test strictly below the lowest blocking\n`block_threshold` (0.60), exactly as `UNDERSPECIFIED_ORACLE_CONTRIB = 0.55` is.\n\n**Required structural change in `impact_plan` (not merely implied by \"mirror oracle-kind\").**\n`impact_plan` today resolves every axis through `_SEV01`, which is keyed exclusively on\n`none|low|medium|high`. The new symbolic grades are absent from that map, so a schema-only change\nwould silently score all three as `0.0` — the axis would stop firing entirely instead of flooring.\nThe named changes, mirroring `ac_unverifiable` / `ORACLE_GRADE01` / `_ORACLE_FLOOR_GRADES` exactly:\n\n1. Add `DIVERGENCE_GRADE01: dict[str | None, float]` mapping `none`→0.0,\n   `incomplete_enumeration`→`DIVERGENCE_INCOMPLETE_CONTRIB`, `contradicts_reality`→1.0,\n   `omits_required_site`→1.0.\n2. Add `_DIVERGENCE_FLOOR_GRADES = (\"contradicts_reality\", \"omits_required_site\")`.\n3. EXCLUDE `divergent_implementation` from the generic `_SEV01` contribution loop and append its\n   `DIVERGENCE_GRADE01` contribution instead — introduce `_PLAN_GRADED_AXES =\n   (\"ac_unverifiable\", \"divergent_implementation\")` as the single name for \"axes graded by a closed\n   kind set\", and drive both the contribution loop and the override loop off it so the two cannot\n   drift apart.\n4. EXCLUDE it from the `_PLAN_HARD_OVERRIDE_AXES` `_SEV01.get(...) > 0.0` check, replacing that\n   with a `_DIVERGENCE_FLOOR_GRADES` membership test OR'd into `has_override`.\n\nThe hard floor stays applied LAST (after the detection amplifier), so a self-revealing divergence\nstill lands at or above 0.85 rather than 0.85 x 0.8 = 0.68.\n\nThe three-way split is required, not cosmetic: the corpus shows a blunt widening would route\n113 findings into the 0.85 floor at once (42 of 983 runs flipping PASS→BLOCK, 4.3%), and the\nLAST time this team widened a grade-blind hard floor it over-fired ~2x and had to be walked\nback (calibration 3: 35.5% of plan-v2 blocks were floor-driven, `ac_unverifiable` 48.9% of\nthem, 56% mere specificity demands). `incomplete_enumeration` is the pressure-release valve\nthat precedent proves is necessary.\n\n### 2. ADR: reject high-cost LLM-based calibration assessments\n\nADR 0054. Records that calibration MUST be driven by the data the gate already produces\nduring normal use (the version-tagged REVIEW_RESULT sidecar corpus) and explicitly REJECTS\npurpose-built LLM eval suites / bulk re-review runs as the calibration instrument, on cost\ngrounds. Narrows ADR 0036's \"A/B gate\" to two permitted zero-marginal-LLM-cost forms:\n(a) offline replay of the existing corpus under the candidate value, and (b) dogfooding the\ncandidate in normal use and observing field results. Supersedes ADR 0036's A/B clause only;\nthe permissive-rollout invariant, version-tagging discipline, and no-pooling-across-versions\nrules all stand.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0054-data-driven-calibration-no-costly-llm-assessments.md` exists, status\n      Accepted, and states (a) the rejection of purpose-built LLM eval suites + bulk re-review\n      as calibration instruments, (b) the two permitted A/B forms (corpus replay, dogfood +\n      observe), and (c) that it narrows ADR 0036's A/B clause while leaving the permissive\n      invariant and version-segmentation intact. Verified: the file exists and\n      `grep -c \"corpus replay\\|dogfood\" docs/adr/0054-*.md` returns >= 2.\n- [ ] ADR 0036 carries a forward pointer to 0054 so a reader arriving at the older A/B clause\n      is not misled. Verified:\n      `grep -n \"0054\" docs/adr/0036-impact-model-rollout-and-calibration-cadence.md` matches.\n- [ ] `PlanSeverityAttrs.divergent_implementation` is a closed `Literal[\"none\",\n      \"contradicts_reality\", \"omits_required_site\", \"incomplete_enumeration\"]` in\n      `src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/verify_models.py`. Verified: a verification payload carrying\n      `\"low\"` for that axis now FAILS to parse (pinned by test).\n- [ ] `impact_plan` resolves the axis through the new `DIVERGENCE_GRADE01` map and NOT through\n      `_SEV01` (per the four named structural changes above): it floors at 0.85 for\n      `contradicts_reality` and `omits_required_site`, and returns\n      `DIVERGENCE_INCOMPLETE_CONTRIB` (never floors) for `incomplete_enumeration`.\n      Verified: unit test asserting all three, plus a test pinning\n      `DIVERGENCE_INCOMPLETE_CONTRIB < 0.60` (the lowest blocking threshold in\n      `criteria_routing.json`) so a future recalibration below it fails loudly.\n- [ ] The target defect now BLOCKS: a regression test replays the exact recorded attributes of\n      finding `fc347224733dccc6b` (validity 0.8889, G6 `block_threshold` 0.60) with\n      `divergent_implementation=\"omits_required_site\"` and asserts `pass3_decide` returns\n      `decision == \"block\"` (priority 0.7556 >= 0.60).\n- [ ] Both verifier prompts (`plan_review_verifier.md`, `plan_review_verifier_agentic.md`)\n      define all three grades with a worked example each, in the same shape as the\n      `ac_unverifiable` oracle-kind block. Verified:\n      `grep -c \"omits_required_site\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_verifier*.md` returns\n      at least 1 per file.\n- [ ] The plan-review `IMPACT_MODEL_VERSION` is bumped to `plan-v4`, and legacy plan-v3/v2\n      sidecars still read without error. Verified: `test_impact_model_versioning.py` pins\n      updated and green.\n- [ ] `enrich_operator_attested` handling is explicit and UNCONDITIONAL for the new grades: on a\n      recorded operator attestation it MUST set `incomplete_enumeration` to `none` (automatic, not\n      discretionary), and MUST NOT alter `contradicts_reality` or `omits_required_site` — they\n      survive enrichment unchanged (mirroring the `broken_oracle` rule). Verified: a unit test\n      asserting the exact post-attestation value of the axis for all three grades\n      (`incomplete_enumeration` → `\"none\"`; each floor grade → itself).\n- [ ] EVERY existing fixture that grades the axis on the retired ordinal ladder is migrated to\n      the closed set — the Literal makes `\"low\"|\"medium\"|\"high\"` a parse error, so a missed\n      fixture is a broken test, and one of them lives OUTSIDE tests/unit. Known sites:\n      `tests/unit/test_a8e5_operator_attested_edge.py` (uses `\"high\"` as a surviving-other-axis\n      fixture) and `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py:1282` (uses `\"high\"` as\n      the hard-override fixture that drives its negative-control BLOCK). Verified:\n      `grep -rn '\"divergent_implementation\": \"\\(high\\|medium\\|low\\)\"' tests/ src/` returns no\n      matches outside the grade-split suite's own rejection test, AND `pytest tests/unit\n      tests/interfaces` is green.\n- [ ] The verifier prompts' shared preamble no longer tells the LLM the axis uses the ordinal\n      ladder (a pink-elephant that would fight the new grades): the \"Grade each axis\n      none|low|medium|high (EXCEPT ac_unverifiable...)\" line names BOTH kind-graded axes.\n      Verified: `grep -c \"EXCEPT ac_unverifiable and divergent_implementation\"\n      src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_verifier*.md` returns 1 per file.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; every touched `src/rebar` file stays under the\n      800-LOC module-size cap. Verified: `make lint typecheck` exits 0.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-gate.md` and the generated `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md`\n      describe the new grades. Verified: the guide is regenerated from the registry, not\n      hand-edited.\n\n## Out of scope (recorded, not done here)\n\n- The `prod_impact` floor (\"Fix 2\") is REJECTED — it collides with the novelty convergence\n  floor (`novelty_priority_floor = 0.4`). See the evaluation comment on this ticket.\n- `undecomposed` fires on 0.07% of plan-v3 findings (12/18,085) while holding hard-override\n  power — a likely dead axis. Filed separately."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785279270008415001,"uuid":"8b18b443-f7e6-460d-a435-38ba3517a61a"}